import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ag as i}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Example: a feature in a Julia package","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agentic/example.md","filePath":"agentic/example.md"}'),n={name:"agentic/example.md"};function o(d,e,r,l,h,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="example-a-feature-in-a-julia-package" tabindex="-1">Example: a feature in a Julia package <a class="header-anchor" href="#example-a-feature-in-a-julia-package" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Example: a feature in a Julia package&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page follows one feature from the first conversation to the merge. The package <code>Pendulum</code> is fictitious: a small Julia package that integrates the equations of a pendulum with a fixed time step. The feature is an adaptive step: the integrator estimates its error and changes the step to keep the error near a tolerance. The commands and the files are those of the harness; the messages of the agents are short illustrations, not real output.</p><h2 id="_1-discuss" tabindex="-1">1. Discuss <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-discuss" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Discuss&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You open a session in the checkout of <code>Pendulum</code> and describe what you want:</p><blockquote><p>Add an adaptive step to <code>Pendulum</code>. The error estimate comes from an embedded pair. The step grows by at most 5 and shrinks by at most 5. Plan it as parts.</p></blockquote><p>The session reads the code before it plans. It finds the integrator in <code>src/integrate.jl</code>, the tests in <code>test/integrate.jl</code>, and the <code>[compat]</code> floor of Julia in <code>Project.toml</code>. It asks you the questions that the code cannot answer, in one round, each with its recommendation:</p><ol><li>Which norm does the error estimate take? <em>Recommendation: the root mean square, scaled by the tolerance.</em></li><li>Does the public function <code>integrate</code> keep its signature, with a new keyword <code>adaptive</code>? <em>Recommendation: yes, with <code>adaptive = false</code>, so that no caller changes.</em></li></ol><p>You answer the second question at once and leave the first open. The session writes a task file, <code>Tasks/Add the adaptive step to Pendulum.md</code>, with <code>status: wip</code>, the goal, the done condition, your answer with your name and the date, and the open question.</p><h2 id="_2-plan" tabindex="-1">2. Plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-plan" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Plan&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The session loads the skill <a href="./../components/skills#plan-parts"><code>plan-parts</code></a>. It splits the work into three parts, each one pull request:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">part</th><th style="text-align:left;">repository</th><th style="text-align:left;">sections</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it is</th><th style="text-align:left;">tier</th><th style="text-align:left;">state</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>A</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>Pendulum</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">spec, §V, §L</td><td style="text-align:left;">The step controller: a factor from an error estimate. A numerical method.</td><td style="text-align:left;">build</td><td style="text-align:left;">open</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>B</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>Pendulum</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">§4, §V, §L</td><td style="text-align:left;">Which norm the error estimate takes. Changes a public answer.</td><td style="text-align:left;">decision</td><td style="text-align:left;">waiting for the user</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>C</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>Pendulum</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">spec, §V, §L</td><td style="text-align:left;">The docs page and the example. Small, with a fast gate.</td><td style="text-align:left;">reviewed</td><td style="text-align:left;">blocked by A</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Part B is a <code>decision</code> part, because only you can choose the norm. The collision map shows that A and C share only <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>, where each adds its own lines, so they do not serialise (<a href="./tasks#the-collision-map">Tasks</a>).</p><h2 id="_3-write-the-spec" tabindex="-1">3. Write the spec <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-write-the-spec" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Write the spec&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Part A is a numerical method, so the planner reads the section <em>A numerical method</em> of the <a href="./edges">edge catalogue</a> and decides each edge that the controller reaches. The spec, in the spec-delta format, holds requirements such as this one:</p><div class="language-markdown vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">markdown</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">### Requirement: The step factor stays in its bounds</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">The controller SHALL return a factor in [0.2, 5.0] for every finite error estimate.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">#### Scenario: A zero error estimate</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> WHEN </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`step_factor(0.0)\`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> is called</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> THEN it returns </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`5.0\`</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> check: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`julia --project -e &#39;using Pendulum; Pendulum.step_factor(0.0) == 5.0 || exit(1)&#39;\`</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> catches: MU1, the upper bound removed: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`test/controller.jl\`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, testset &quot;bounds&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>In the spec-delta format each decided edge is a scenario of the delta, with its check. The edges of part A, in short:</p><ul><li>an error estimate of <code>NaN</code> or <code>Inf</code>: the factor is <code>0.2</code>, the smallest step;</li><li>an error estimate of <code>0.0</code>: the factor is <code>5.0</code>, the largest step;</li><li><code>Float32</code> input: the factor is a <code>Float32</code>; every constant is converted with <code>T(…)</code>;</li><li>a subnormal error estimate: treated as <code>0.0</code>.</li></ul><p>The <code>catches:</code> lines and the mutant table of the spec name the mutants that the tests must catch, such as MU1, the upper bound removed, and MU2, the <code>NaN</code> branch deleted.</p><p>Two checks run before the build (<a href="./specs-and-gates#the-premise-check">Specs and gates</a>). A <code>reader</code> agent measures each fact of the spec against the code, and finds one false fact: the integrator is in <code>src/integrate.jl</code>, not in <code>src/step.jl</code> as the first draft said. The planner fixes it. Then <code>spec-gate.py</code> runs <code>spec-validate.py</code>, which runs every premise on the base commit:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">cd</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/Research</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> &amp;&amp; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">python3</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> Harness/scripts/spec-gate.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Tasks/Add the adaptive step to Pendulum.md&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> A</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Its first line is <code>A PASS</code>, followed by the validator&#39;s lines, and planning ends.</p><h2 id="_4-build" tabindex="-1">4. Build <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Build&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You start the loop:</p><blockquote><p>Build part A.</p></blockquote><p>The skill <a href="./../components/skills#build-part"><code>build-part</code></a> writes the spec bundle of part A and spawns <code>julia-builder</code> in a new worktree, <code>~/Research/.worktrees/Pendulum-agent-&lt;id&gt;</code>. The builder writes the tests first, then <code>step_factor</code>, then runs <code>run-tests.jl affected</code>, and the named mutants and its own mutants with <code>mutate.jl</code>. It returns:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Verdict: ready for critic</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Mutants: MU1 CAUGHT, MU2 CAUGHT; own: 6 CAUGHT, 0 SURVIVED</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Round 1.</strong> Two <code>julia-critic</code> agents judge the branch at high effort, each blind to the other and to the builder, while <code>julia-test-runner</code> runs the full suite. Because part A has a spec in the spec-delta format, the round also runs the spec&#39;s checks on the head and pushes the branch for CI. One critic passes. The other fails the part with one blocking defect:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Verdict: FAIL</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Blocking: wrong result — step_factor(1.0f-3) returns a Float64.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  Reproducer: julia -e &#39;using Pendulum; Pendulum.step_factor(1.0f-3) isa Float32 || exit(1)&#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  The safety factor 0.9 is a Float64 literal, against the decided edge &quot;Float32 input&quot;.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The round fails, because either critic can fail it.</p><p><strong>The fix round.</strong> A new builder gets the defect and the last builder&#39;s report, writes the failing test first, then converts the constant with <code>T(0.9)</code>. It reruns the mutants that the fix touches, and returns <code>ready for critic</code>.</p><p><strong>The verify round.</strong> One new critic judges only the fix: it gets the earlier blocking defect and its reproducer, never the builder&#39;s report. It reruns the reproducer, the tests that the fix reaches, and MU1 and MU2. The suite is green, and the critic says <code>PASS</code>.</p><p><strong>The finish.</strong> A new builder rereads the text of the branch, runs <code>julia-branch-verifier</code>, stages the paths that the verifier names, and opens the pull request with <code>gh pr create -R &lt;owner&gt;/Pendulum</code>. <code>build-part</code> writes <code>PR #12 in review</code> into the parts table, and records the rounds: two critic rounds, no advisor decision, no ruling.</p><p>The defect of round 1 is inside the domain of a decided edge, so it adds nothing to the edge catalogue. Had the critic found a failure outside every clause, for example on an error estimate of <code>-0.0</code>, it would have recorded it as found late, and <code>build-part</code> would have added its class to the catalogue after the loop.</p><h2 id="_5-decide-and-review" tabindex="-1">5. Decide and review <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-decide-and-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Decide and review&quot;">​</a></h2><p>While part A is in review, you answer the question of part B: the root mean square, scaled by the tolerance. The session writes the decision into the task file with your name and the date, writes the spec of part B in the spec-delta format, sets its <code>sections</code> cell to <code>spec, §V, §L</code>, and, with your yes, gives it the tier <code>reviewed</code>, because nothing in it is open any more.</p><p>CI runs on the pull request of part A: the tests on Julia <code>min</code> and <code>1</code> on three systems, the downgrade job and the doctests (<a href="./tests-and-ci#the-ci-of-a-package">Tests and CI</a>). You read the diff and the evidence table of the critic, and merge on GitHub.</p><h2 id="_6-the-rest-of-the-task" tabindex="-1">6. The rest of the task <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-the-rest-of-the-task" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. The rest of the task&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Part C is now unblocked. You ask for it:</p><blockquote><p>Build part C.</p></blockquote><p>The skill <a href="./../components/skills#build-reviewed"><code>build-reviewed</code></a> spawns one <code>part-builder</code> and one <code>part-critic</code>. The critic passes the docs page at once, your session reads the result, and the builder opens the pull request. Part B goes the same way.</p><p>When the three pull requests are merged, the done condition holds. The session writes <code>status: done</code> into the task file and moves it into <code>Tasks/Archive/</code>.</p><h2 id="what-the-example-shows" tabindex="-1">What the example shows <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-example-shows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the example shows&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The questions came first.</strong> Each question that only you could answer was asked before the build, in one round. The loop then ran without a question to you.</li><li><strong>The spec decided the edge, and the critic held the code to it.</strong> The <code>Float32</code> defect was not a matter of taste: a line of the spec named the answer.</li><li><strong>Each judgement came from a new context.</strong> No critic saw a builder&#39;s reasoning, and the verify round saw only the fix.</li><li><strong>You merged.</strong> No agent of the loop merged its own pull request (<a href="./review#who-merges">Review and pull requests</a>).</li></ul>`,43)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
